Date: Tue, 23 Nov 1993 00:26:43 -0800
From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Subject: E-Prime - a dumb question

   Perhaps this is overly simplistic, but what is the point of E-prime?
   It seems to be to unpack language and make explicit many assumptions
   that are implicit.  Once these assumptions are pointed out why bother
   to repeat them each time you make a statement?  Sounds like E' is
   something worth knowing about but not practising.

   E' reduces the baud rate.

   Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

Often as I translate a sentence into E', _I_ get a clearer idea of
what I mean.  I value clear thinking, so I plan to keep using it.
Besides, sometimes I shorten sentences by translating them.
(Case in point:  I translated this from
"Besides, sometimes sentences are shorter after I translate them.")

I find the active voice more powerful than the passive voice (E' "X
wrote the book", vs. E "the book was written").

Also, in my experience "I think X" causes fewer arguments, or at least 
fewer flame wars, than "X is true", notwithstanding that others may
have understood any implicit assumptions involved.

There you have three of my reasons for continuing to use E'.  Your
mileage may vary.

