Date: Tue, 23 Nov 1993 9:56:58 +0000 (GMT)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: Serial numbers on currency (was Tech: Truth

Tim Freeman <tsf@cs.cmu.edu> writes:

>From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
>>I'd be willing to bet that if you can come up with some of the right
>>kind of paper and a sample banknote you'd have little difficulty, for
>>example, copying whatever they use in Uzbekistan on a copier sold in the
>>US.  (Or even in programming a workstation to print copies with
>>sequentially changed index numbers.)

>Hmm.  If the currency maker has any sense, there is some cryptography
>involved in the serial numbers to make it easy for banks and others to
>check that a serial number is valid, but hard for anybody to generate
>a valid serial number without copying an existing bill.

>But such a scheme would have to be well-advertised to be useful, and I
>haven't seen the advertisement.  Has anybody else?  Or does anybody
>know for sure that the serial numbers on US $ are sequential?

Well, it's a matter of public record that Bank of England banknotes
use an encryption system to generate their numbers. (There was an
incident ages ago, during WW2, when a rather demented SS officer set
up a scheme to destroy the Sterling system by flooding England with
forged twenty-pound notes (then worth as much as a 500-pound note
today :). The scheme came to nothing, but they _did_ succeed in
cracking the encryption scheme (by obtaining a large quantity of
currency.) 

I seem to recall (YMMV) that some mints -- the Royal Mint, notably --
subcontract for foreign governments who don't have the infrastructure
to design and produce their own currency. So quite a few third world
currencies will have encrypted banknote ID's that will be forgery
resistant. 

However, as a matter of theory, if I was looking for a target currency
I'd go for one with a high inflation rate. Firstly, the government
would have worse problems to worry about than forgery; they'd be
printing their own notes to save foreign exchange. Secondly, the
lifespan of a note in circulation would be decreased, so there'd be a
lower chance of a forgery being detected and traced. Thirdly, while
reissuing currencies every four weeks a government is likely to either
use the same encryption system to generate index numbers (just adding
some zeroes) or to forgo encryption altogether. Just take last weeks'
trash and copy the numbers with a high speed scanner.

I'd be very surprised if any currency with an inflation rate >1200%
wasn't an automatic target for high-tech forgers. Just another reason
why we need digital cash, NOW. And just another reason why I expect
lots of people -- possibly including myself -- to lose their shirts in
the crypto phase-change.



-- Charlie
