>that say more about the reporters than anything else.

Absolutely.

More to the point; there's a big difference between a manager and a team
leader, if the organization supports that kind of structure.  The idea,
I suppose, is that management denotes paternalism, rigid
hierarchicalism, patronage, and _management_.  A team leader on the
other hand trusts their skilled workers to manage themselves, and
concentrates on leading a team towards a well-defined goal.  Looney left
political correctness?  Hell no!  High-tech industrial leading edge
correctness, more like.

Unfortunately most journalistic stereotypes are still rooted in the
memetic detritus of the nineteenth century; journalists
don't work in an area where they get first-hand experience of modern
industrial management principles.


