Date: Tue, 23 Nov 1993 12:43:39 -0500 (EST)
From: LEVY%BESSIE@venus.cis.yale.edu
Subject: E-prime: a dumb question

Mike Price writes:

>Perhaps this is overly simplistic, but what is the point of E-prime?
>It seems to be to unpack language and make explicit many assumptions
>that are implicit.  Once these assumptions are pointed out why bother
>to repeat them each time you make a statement?  Sounds like E' is
>something worth knowing about but not practising.

No, this is not a dumb question at all!  In fact, I think it may be the most 
important criticism of E' that one can make.  

It is probably a property of our thinking about the world, and not a property 
of our language, that we have a way of predicating qualities to entities ("John 
is tall.").  So we may always be making the assumptions that Mike mentions, 
even if we avoid "be". 

On the other hand, I still believe that E' is worth practising, because it 
reduces the opportunity to make ambiguous, unverifiable statements.  It's not 
harder to lie in E', but it's easier to get caught.  

I discuss these issues in my upcoming _Extropy_ article.

-- Simon!


