Harvey Newstrom:
>>Cryptanalysis is a developing technology...
>>o what's to say ten years down the line, PGP won't be easy to break?
>
>Exactly.  PGP and other crypto methods are based on the time it takes to 
>perform calculations.  A magnitude increase in computer technology will 
>make craking PGP easier.  In the very-long-term, when computer arrays, or 
>time machines become possible, all messages are almost guaranteed to be 
>cracked.  How far in the futre does it have to be until you don't care?
 
        Seems to me that no secret, state or otherwise is terribly safe
when you're talking time machines, but what makes PGP-type methods
attractive is that they're based on factoring large numbers -- a problem
which is (as far as I know) NP.  Unless P = NP -- which is believed to be
pretty unlikely --, we the cryptographers have the upper hand.

        Why?  Because while adding a digit to the length of the number
being factored represents an order of magnitude difference in time expected
to perform the factoring, it's trivial for the cryptoware to deal with.

        Corrections, as always, welcome.

        -o
        
        Oh.. yeah.  I'd expect the volume of encrypted messages to far
outpace any increases in computational power, so if not for that reason
alone, the federal letter-readers are drinking out of a firehose.



